# Row-Level Security (RLS)

Implement data-level access control using filter policies to restrict which records users can access.

## Prerequisites

- Bknd project with code-first configuration
- Auth enabled (`auth: { enabled: true }`)
- Guard enabled (`guard: { enabled: true }`)
- Roles `user` and `manager` defined (see **bknd-create-role**)
- Entities with ownership and team fields (e.g., `assigned_to`, `team_id`)

## When to Use UI Mode

- Viewing current role policies
- Quick policy inspection

**UI steps:** Admin Panel > Auth > Roles > Select role

**Note:** RLS configuration requires code mode. UI is read-only.

## When to Use Code Mode

- Implementing row-level security
- Creating filter policies
- Entity-specific data isolation
- Multi-tenant patterns

## Code Approach

### Step 1: Define Schema with Ownership Fields

Ensure entities have fields for ownership and team:

```typescript
import { serve } from "bknd/adapter/bun";
import { em, entity, text, number, boolean } from "bknd";

const schema = em({
  tasks: entity("tasks", {
    title: text().required(),
    status: text(),
    assigned_to: number().required(),
    team_id: number().required(),
    is_public: boolean().default(false),
  }),
  projects: entity("projects", {
    name: text().required(),
    owner_id: number().required(),
  }),
});
```

### Step 2: Basic RLS - Users See Only Assigned Tasks

Users can only read tasks where `assigned_to` matches their user ID:

```typescript
serve({
  connection: { url: "file:data.db" },
  config: {
    data: schema.toJSON(),
    auth: {
      enabled: true,
      guard: { enabled: true },
      roles: {
        user: {
          implicit_allow: false,
          permissions: [
            {
              permission: "data.entity.read",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  description: "Users read only their assigned tasks",
                  effect: "filter",
                  filter: { assigned_to: "@user.id" },
                },
                // Projects: everyone can read
                {
                  condition: { entity: "projects" },
                  effect: "allow",
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
        },
      },
    },
  },
});
```

### How Filter Policies Work

| Component | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `effect: "filter"` | Apply row-level filtering (not allow/deny) |
| `filter` | Query conditions added to every request |
| `@user.id` | Variable replaced with current user's ID |

When user with ID 5 queries tasks, the filter transforms:
```typescript
// User's query
api.data.readMany("tasks", { where: { status: "active" } });

// Becomes (with RLS filter applied)
api.data.readMany("tasks", { where: { status: "active", assigned_to: 5 } });
```

### Step 3: Full CRUD with RLS for Users

Apply RLS to all operations. Users can create tasks (assigned_to is set by the client) and update/delete only their own assigned tasks:

```typescript
{
  roles: {
    user: {
      implicit_allow: false,
      permissions: [
        // Read: own assigned tasks (no public tasks)
        {
          permission: "data.entity.read",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [
            {
              condition: { entity: "tasks" },
              effect: "filter",
              filter: { assigned_to: "@user.id" },
            },
            {
              condition: { entity: "projects" },
              effect: "allow",
            },
          ],
        },
        // Create: allowed (client must send assigned_to = user.id)
        { permission: "data.entity.create", effect: "allow" },
        // Update: only own tasks
        {
          permission: "data.entity.update",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { assigned_to: "@user.id" },
          }],
        },
        // Delete: only own tasks
        {
          permission: "data.entity.delete",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { assigned_to: "@user.id" },
          }],
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```

### Step 4: Manager Role - Team-Based Access

Managers can see all tasks within their team (based on `@user.team_id`):

```typescript
{
  roles: {
    manager: {
      implicit_allow: false,
      permissions: [
        {
          permission: "data.entity.read",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [
            {
              condition: { entity: "tasks" },
              effect: "filter",
              filter: { team_id: "@user.team_id" },
            },
            {
              condition: { entity: "projects" },
              effect: "allow",
            },
          ],
        },
        // Create, update, delete: only tasks in their team
        {
          permission: "data.entity.create",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "tasks" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { team_id: "@user.team_id" },
          }],
        },
        {
          permission: "data.entity.update",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "tasks" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { team_id: "@user.team_id" },
          }],
        },
        {
          permission: "data.entity.delete",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "tasks" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { team_id: "@user.team_id" },
          }],
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```

### Step 5: Projects Entity - Owner-Only Edit

Everyone can read projects, but only the owner can update/delete:

```typescript
{
  roles: {
    user: {
      permissions: [
        // Read projects: allowed without filter
        {
          permission: "data.entity.read",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [
            {
              condition: { entity: "projects" },
              effect: "allow",
            },
          ],
        },
        // Update/delete projects: only owner
        {
          permission: "data.entity.update",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "projects" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { owner_id: "@user.id" },
          }],
        },
        {
          permission: "data.entity.delete",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "projects" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { owner_id: "@user.id" },
          }],
        },
      ],
    },
    manager: {
      // Same as user for projects - owner-only edit
      permissions: [
        {
          permission: "data.entity.read",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [
            {
              condition: { entity: "projects" },
              effect: "allow",
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          permission: "data.entity.update",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "projects" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { owner_id: "@user.id" },
          }],
        },
        {
          permission: "data.entity.delete",
          effect: "allow",
          policies: [{
            condition: { entity: "projects" },
            effect: "filter",
            filter: { owner_id: "@user.id" },
          }],
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```

## How `@user.team_id` Works

The `@user.team_id` variable assumes the authenticated user object has a `team_id` field. This must be set at registration or via a custom hook. For example:

```typescript
// During registration, you must include team_id in metadata
await api.auth.register({
  email: "manager@test.com",
  password: "pass123",
  metadata: { team_id: 1 },
});
```

Then the variable is resolved from the user's `metadata.team_id`.

## Verification

### 1. Create Test Users

```bash
# User 1 (team 1)
curl -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/auth/password/register \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user1@test.com", "password": "pass123", "metadata": {"team_id": 1}}'

# User 2 (team 2)
curl -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/auth/password/register \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user2@test.com", "password": "pass123", "metadata": {"team_id": 2}}'

# Manager (team 1)
curl -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/auth/password/register \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "manager@test.com", "password": "pass123", "metadata": {"team_id": 1}}'
```

### 2. Create Tasks as User 1

```bash
# Login as user1
TOKEN1=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/auth/password/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user1@test.com", "password": "pass123"}' | jq -r '.token')

# Create task (must set assigned_to manually)
curl -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/data/tasks \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN1" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title": "User1 Task", "assigned_to": 1, "team_id": 1, "is_public": false}'
```

### 3. Verify RLS as User 2

```bash
# Login as user2
TOKEN2=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/auth/password/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user2@test.com", "password": "pass123"}' | jq -r '.token')

# Query tasks - should see only user2's tasks (not user1's)
curl http://localhost:7654/api/data/tasks \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN2"
# Expected: empty array (user2 has no tasks)
```

### 4. Verify Manager Access

```bash
# Login as manager (team 1)
TOKENM=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/auth/password/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "manager@test.com", "password": "pass123"}' | jq -r '.token')

# Query tasks - should see all tasks with team_id = 1
curl http://localhost:7654/api/data/tasks \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKENM"
# Expected: user1's task (team 1) visible, user2's task (team 2) not visible
```

### 5. Verify Projects Owner Update

```bash
# User1 creates a project
curl -X POST http://localhost:7654/api/data/projects \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN1" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "User1 Project", "owner_id": 1}'

# User2 tries to update - should fail
curl -X PATCH http://localhost:7654/api/data/projects/1 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN2" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "Hacked!"}'
# Expected: 0 rows affected (filtered by owner_id)
```

## Common Pitfalls

### Missing Public Task Visibility

Users will **not** see public tasks unless you add an `$or` condition. The example above only filters by `assigned_to`, so public tasks assigned to other users are hidden.

### Automatic Ownership Not Set

The `assigned_to` field must be explicitly provided by the client on creation. There is no automatic hook to set it to the user's ID. Always ensure the client sends the correct value.

### Manager Sees Only Team Tasks, Not Own

If a manager is also assigned a task, they will see it because it's in their team, but the filter does not include `assigned_to: @user.id` as an alternative. The current configuration relies solely on `team_id`.

### Projects Entity: Manager Edit Restriction

Managers cannot edit projects they don't own, even if the project belongs to their team. The `owner_id` filter applies to all roles.

## DOs and DON'Ts

**DO:**
- Include `team_id` in user metadata for multi-team scenarios
- Use `condition: { entity: "..." }` for entity-specific policies
- Test with multiple users and roles to verify isolation
- Manually set ownership fields (e.g., `assigned_to`) on create

**DON'T:**
- Forget to add `team_id` to user metadata
- Assume public tasks are visible to everyone (need `$or` filter)
- Mix `effect: "allow"` with `filter` (use `effect: "filter"`)
- Overlook that managers need their own tasks visibility too

## Related Skills

- **bknd-create-role** - Define roles for RLS
- **bknd-assign-permissions** - Configure role permissions
- **bknd-protect-endpoint** - Secure specific endpoints
- **bknd-public-vs-auth** - Public vs authenticated access
- **bknd-crud-read** - Query data with filters
